N2  -  The sensitivity of acoustic emissions (AE) techniques in detecting changes in pulsed Tungsten Inert Gas (TIG) welding process parameters, combined with their versatility and ability to extract relevant information, makes them an effective non-destructive technique (NDT) for integration into simulation tools for real-time process monitoring or control. In this study, the incidence of the variation of the pulsed TIG welding process parameters on the AE signal generated by the arc was investigated, with the aim of modeling and simulating this process. Welded joints of AISI316L steel were obtained for three different levels of welding current, 40, 60 and 80 A, and arc length (3, 5 and 7 mm), respectively. It was demonstrated that the changes in arc length and welding current, i.e., the variation of the energy input of the process, have a significant and directly proportional influence on the energy of the AE signal generated by the arc, which would allow the use of AE signal for integration into real-time process monitoring and simulation tools. The frequency band for which the AE coming from the arc is produced during TIG welding process is around 25 kHz, distant from the noise produced in industrial environments that usually occurs at low frequencies (3 - 10 kHz). This behavior allows the use of the AE technique in the monitoring and control of pulsed TIG welding processes, even in noisy environments.  © 2025 The Authors. Published by Elsevier B.V.

