N2  -  The combustion processes in gas turbines are non-linear and complex. These processes also involve interactions among multiple physics. Traditional simulations developed to study these processes often rely on numerical techniques, which suffer from high computational costs, poor real-time capability, and limited emission prediction accuracy. This paper presents a method for modeling gas turbine combustion processes and optimizing emissions that utilizes a physics-informed neural network (PINN). The first step involves establishing physical constraints, which include equations for turbulent reactive flows, energy conservation, and chemical kinetics, and training the neural network by coupling with the measurement data. Next, a strategy for dynamic weight adjustment is developed in order to improve the balance between the data fitting and the constraints for the physical equations. The emission prediction model is finally used to couple with a multi-objective optimization algorithm aimed at optimally controlling emissions of nitrogen oxides (NOx) and carbon monoxide (CO) while maximizing combustion efficiency. In practical examples, the PINN model improved computational efficiency by approximately 13.5 times compared to traditional computational fluid dynamics (CFD) approaches for predicting distribution of combustion temperatures. The error in the combustor outlet temperature prediction was kept within 2.5% resulting in a 1.9% improvement in combustion efficiency.  © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).

