N2  -  Decontamination operations are critical for countering chemical, biological, radiological, and nuclear (CBRN) threats. Their complexity, high cost, and inherent risk make computational Modeling and Simulation (M&S) a core tool for researching, training, and evaluating these missions. This review adopts a technology-driven perspective to systematically survey the development of computational technologies underpinning decontamination operation simulation. It begins by deconstructing the physico-chemical processes and military procedures that form the basis of simulation. The core discussion focuses on the evolution and integration of three pivotal computational modeling paradigms: high-fidelity environmental modeling, mechanism-driven process modeling, and intelligent agent-based behavioral modeling. Furthermore, it explores technical approaches for integrating these heterogeneous models using distributed simulation standards like the High-Level Architecture (HLA), detailing data exchange mechanisms and time synchronization via the Runtime Infrastructure (RTI). Finally, the review outlines future computational challenges and trends, including digital twins and AI-driven surrogate modeling. This synthesis aims to provide developers of decontamination simulation systems with a clear understanding of the computational technological trajectory and a practical framework for system construction.  © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).

