N2  -  The prediction of combustion processes is a highly nonlinear, multivariable challenge with significant implications for energy efficiency and safety. This paper proposes a deep learning-driven framework that tightly integrates computational simulation and neural modeling by combining eddy current simulation with an Attention-based neural network architecture. The eddy current simulation provides high-resolution spatiotemporal data on flow dynamics and thermal distribution, forming the computational foundation for data-driven modeling. The Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) module performs spatial feature extraction from the simulated data, while the Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) module captures temporal dependencies in combustion evolution. To enhance feature discrimination, an attention mechanism is incorporated, allowing the model to focus on critical moments during the combustion process. The proposed framework is trained using the AdamW optimizer, which decouples weight decay from gradient updates, thus improving convergence and generalization. Experiments conducted on a large-scale combustion dataset demonstrate that our method outperforms traditional neural architectures. The results validate the effectiveness of integrating physics-based simulation and deep learning, offering a robust computational strategy for combustion process modeling in intelligent energy systems and industrial control.  © 2025 The Authors.

