N2  -  Co-rotating twin-screw extruders are one of the most important processing machines in the polymer industry, and in many related such as pharmaceutical, chemical, and food industry. Modelling the processing behaviour is commonly based on analytical approaches building on significant simplifications, such as approximating kneading blocks by conveying elements and considering a flat channel approximation. The most important conveying parameter is the drag-flow capacity, which mainly influences the degree of filling and the distribution of the processed material inside the extruder. In this work, we present a novel generalized regression model, with which it is for the first time possible to analytically predict the drag-flow rate of fully-intermeshing co-rotating twin-screw extruder kneading blocks considering the accurate three-dimensional flow geometry. Therefore, we first analysed the problem and governing equations, then applied the theory of similarity to identify the characteristic influencing parameters and reduce the parameter space, next conducted a parametrically driven numerical design study, and finally developed the symbolic regression models based on the simulated data. The highly accurate model allows fast, stable, and accurate prediction of the drag-flow capacity, thus contributing to optimal design of extruder screws without the need for numerical simulations. Copyright © 2022 The Authors.

